WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.800
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to "DataVis DevTalk."

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.170
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, developer advocate

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.790
for Data Studio.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.892
In this episode of date
of "DataVis DevTalk,"

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:09.600
you will learn about
different approaches

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.050
to build custom solutions
with BigQuery and Data Studio.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:13.800
Let's get started.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.768
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.920
BigQuery is Google's
fully-managed petabytes scale

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.530
low cost data
warehousing solution.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.110
Data Studio natively
integrates with BigQuery,

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.990
and can be used to analyze
and visualize BigQuery data.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.430
You can also use a new BigQuery
BI engine with Data Studio

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.880
to build rich interactive
dashboards and reports

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.480
without compromising
performance, scale, security

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.890
or data freshness.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.440
When you're trying to
build a reporting solution

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.960
from your BigQuery data,
there are several approaches

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.250
you can take.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.860
In this video, we'll talk about
the recommended approaches

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.310
for different use cases.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.760
You can use the native BigQuery
connector in Data Studio

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.460
to fetch and visualize
BigQuery create data.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.040
You can fetch entire
cables from BigQuery

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.310
or run custom queries
within Data Studio.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.740
This approach is
useful if your users

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.370
are doing exploratory analysis
and are familiar with SQL

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.670
and can write their own queries,
and are familiar with the data

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.210
and know how to visualize
it from scratch.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.900
When using the native BigQuery
connector, you or your users

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.750
can also take advantage
of the Explorer feature

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.500
indeed Data Studio to
visually construct the queries

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.720
without writing custom SQL.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.750
Keep in mind, that your users
will need a BigQuery billing

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.250
account to use the
native BigQuery

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.290
connector in Data Studio UI.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.750
There are other use cases
when you might need a more

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.760
customized solution.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.030
One use case is where you want
to incorporate existing queries

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.420
into your connector.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.960
Your users won't have
to write their own SQL

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.710
or copy paste SQL snippets
to get the exact query.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.840
Additionally, you can
parameterize your queries

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.440
and let your users provide input
via the connector configuration

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.100
to customize the queries.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.760
Another use case
is when you want

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.520
to use service accounts
to centralize billing.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.590
This means your users
will not need access

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.910
to a GCB billing account to
query and visualize their data.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.610
Other use cases include
getting your users started

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.240
with template reports
populated with their own data,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.250
and implementing your
own cache in there

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.350
to control BigQuery cost.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.770
In all these cases, it is useful
to create a custom solution

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.810
with a community connector.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.150
In a community connector,
you can access BigQuery

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.110
in three separate ways--

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.180
Data Studio Advanced
Services, BigQuery REST API,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.880
and Apps Script
BigQuery Service.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.820
Most of the custom use cases
can be solved with Data Studio

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.500
Advanced Services.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.570
While using the service
in your connector,

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.240
you provide a query
configuration in the code.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.550
Based on this
configuration, Data Studio

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.670
will fetch the data for you
using its native connection

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:54.480
to BigQuery.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.180
This works as fast as the
native BigQuery connector,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.580
since the data does not
pass through Apps Script

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.910
and does not require any
additional transformation.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.770
Any filters added
to the dashboard

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.840
are automatically
pushed down to BigQuery,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.340
so the connector
does not get bogged

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.290
down with handling excess data.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.180
This approach requires
the least amount

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.490
of code among the
three separate ways,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.250
since you're passing only
configuration to Data Studio.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.880
The World Bank example connector
in our open source repo

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.190
uses Data Studio
Advanced Services.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.350
Link is in the description,
if you want to see the code.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.930
We will have a
separate video showing

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.840
how you can use the Data
Studio Advanced Services when

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.240
building your own connector.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.400
When you use the Data
Studio Advanced Services,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.440
the connector code
does not get access

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.720
to the data being
fetched from BigQuery,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.850
so it is not possible to
implement your own caching

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:42.600
there.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.780
You also cannot do additional
transformations or calculations

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.940
with the data.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.370
In these cases, you
can use the App Script

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.350
URL fetch app service to
access the BigQuery REST API.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:59.070
This approach lets you send off
your query to the BigQuery REST

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:03.510
endpoint and get back your
query results in your connector.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.480
You can then implement
custom caching

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.400
or do additional
transformation, as required.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.870
For an example of BigQuery
REST API implementation,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.550
check out the
[INAUDIBLE] connector.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:14.900
Link is in the description.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.560
Apps Script BigQuery
Service is useful

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.640
when you need to do manual
calculation or cross

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.050
formation in your connector, but
are hitting the URL fetch app

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.913
limits due to large data sizes.

00:04:24.913 --> 00:04:26.580
This approach does
have its limitations,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.670
since you cannot use the service
account to access BigQuery this

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.230
way.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.320
To quickly summarize, use
the native BigQuery connector

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.620
to create dashboards when your
users are exploring the data

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.380
and are familiar with
the data set and SQL.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.900
For custom solutions,
create community connectors

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.820
to access BigQuery.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.860
Data Studio Advanced
Services should

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.170
address most of the issues,
unless you need access

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.060
to the data in your code.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.680
In such cases, use the BigQuery
REST API or the Apps Script

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.100
BigQuery Service.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.200
All of these approaches
support calculated fields

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.840
in the schema, and can
use templated reports

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:01.800
with the connector.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.870
To learn more, check out the
links in the description below.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.870
And don't forget to
subscribe to the GCB channel

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.210
for more great content
on Data Studio.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.985
Thanks for watching,
and until next time,

00:05:10.985 --> 00:05:12.360
keep building
awesome dashboards.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

